WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.779
Deadpool 2 is slicing its way across the big
screen, cutting down the competition to assume

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:05.980
the box office throne.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.320
Like any good blockbuster, it only got there
by trimming its own fat to become as tight

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.400
and lean as it could be.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.500
Finding the perfect balance of raunchy chuckles,
slam-bang action, and emotional payoff required

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.590
a certain amount of trial and error, and now
the Deadpool creators have dropped hints about

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.260
the scenes that were cut from the film.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.430
Here's a taste of what didn't make it into
the theatrical version of Deadpool 2.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:27.880
Warning, spoilers ahead.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:29.560
Goodbye Hitler

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.930
Director David Leitch told Esquire that originally,
a post-credits scene found the time-traveling

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:38.640
Deadpool in a nursery where baby Adolf Hitler
sleeps in his crib.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:42.860
Deadpool rallies his strength and decides
to smother the future fuhrer, which apparently

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.670
left test screening audiences feeling somewhat
uneasy.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:49.820
Ultimately, the filmmakers realized it wasn't
a great way to send people out of the theater.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.420
"Sure, it's baby Hitler, but it is a baby."

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.560
Chris cross cameo

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.140
For another post-credits gag, writers Rhett
Reese and Paul Wernick conceived of a bonus

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.280
round of rejected X-Force auditions.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.220
According to Uproxx, one scripted version
would have had Chris Evans reprise his role

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.380
as the Human Torch from 2005's Fantastic Four.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.280
Given that Evans has always been game to play
jokey cameos, there's a good chance he would

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:13.750
have accepted the opportunity.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:18.360
However, the idea was dropped before any offers
were made, in favor of the time travel shenanigans

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.380
we got instead.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.280
A pool for 'Pool

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.920
Though we lose her early on in the film, there
are a number of scenes in which Vanessa appears

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.860
and speaks to Wade from the afterlife.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:31.580
However, one dream sequence that had been
particularly difficult to shoot ended up on

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.080
the cutting room floor.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.020
Morena Baccarin told Good Morning America
that the scene took place in a version of

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.700
Vanessa and Wade's apartment that was completely
submerged in water.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.700
The filmmakers constructed an aquatic set
and put the actors through scuba training.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:49.040
Baccarin, who has a phobia of being underwater,
had to be strapped to a chair while submerged.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:50.960
"Were you supposed to be terrified?

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.420
… No, and I think that's why it didn't work."

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:57.480
Try harder

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.560
After the loss of Vanessa, Wade's grief weighs
on him so heavily that he tries to do the

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.440
one thing his mutant healing abilities won't
allow him to do: end his own life.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.500
This was the catalyst for a major bit of comedy
that ended up not making the final cut.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:15.219
Reese and Wernick revealed during a Reddit
AMA that, at one stage, there was a montage

00:02:15.219 --> 00:02:19.099
of his increasingly absurd attempts to off
himself, in which he tried everything from

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.060
drinking Drano to jumping into a polar bear
enclosure.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:26.080
Despite the scrapping of the idea, the scenes
were shot and are likely to surface on the

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.640
eventual Blu-ray release.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:29.360
Gamblin' man

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.079
Back when they were working on the first drafts
of the sequel, Reese and Wernick told Business

00:02:34.079 --> 00:02:38.900
Insider they hoped to include a previously-shot
scene in which Deadpool gives a rousing karaoke

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.940
performance of Kenny Rogers' "The Gambler"
- while taking down a gang of bad guys.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.040
Wernick confessed to being so delighted by
Reynolds' performance that he kept the video

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:47.620
on his phone.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.160
The writers' had originally hoped to include
it in the sequel - but the gamble has yet

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:51.680
to pay off.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:53.560
Early drafts

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:59.240
Reynolds has hinted that a large foundational
change was made to Deadpool 2 during its development.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:00.960
He confirmed to Entertainment Weekly,

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:05.620
"In early drafts, we had Deadpool as a dad,
and by page, I think, two-and-a-half, we realized

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:06.900
that's a terrible idea."

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.960
Reynolds also claimed that one, quote, "sore
spot" about the making of Deadpool 2 was Fox's

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.820
request to remove a joke made at the expense
of Disney.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:19.540
He wouldn't explain what the joke was, but
it's hard not to wonder if the potential Fox/Disney

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.340
merger had something to do with the cutting
of the scene.

00:03:22.469 --> 00:03:26.599
The screenwriters told CBR that there was
another early draft with much more Black Tom

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:27.100
Cassidy.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:31.319
He was intended to survive to the end of the
movie and play a part in the climactic orphanage

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:31.880
showdown.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.960
The reduction of his presence was mainly a
matter of budget, but Reese consoled fans

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:36.460
by adding,

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.160
"We can always go back in time and make anybody
alive, so perhaps he'll come back."

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.100
Extended scenes

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:47.060
Director David Leitch was aware that he couldn't
just lean on senseless violence for the sequel,

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.680
and he tried to be mindful of how much was
too much.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.920
He told Bloody Disgusting that the opening
montage in which Wade mows down a bathhouse

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.260
full of yakuza was much more intense before
it left the cutting room.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:02.420
He described a, quote, "seamless 360 shot"
that he was particularly sorry to lose.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:06.280
Another scene that only partly made it into
the film includes two delightfully surprising

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.260
cameos hidden in plain sight.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:13.329
Behind prosthetics and thick fake accents
are Matt Damon and Alan Tudyk as two country

00:04:13.329 --> 00:04:16.100
boys debating the merits of proper bathroom
hygiene.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.540
Josh Brolin revealed in a Collider interview
that this scene was, quote, "cut in half."

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:24.800
With cameos like these, it's safe to assume
the rest of the scene will turn up eventually.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:26.160
Thanks for watching!

